# Week 4 — Urdu OCR Model Training

Fine-tuning a pretrained TrOCR model on the Urdu OCR dataset.

## Why This Matters

Traditional OCR systems can struggle with Urdu text. Fine-tuning a pretrained OCR model allows the model to learn from Urdu image-text examples and adapt to the Urdu OCR task.

## Live Demo

[Urdu OCR Hugging Face Space](PASTE_YOUR_HUGGINGFACE_SPACE_LINK_HERE)

## How It Works

The project uses Microsoft's pretrained TrOCR model. TrOCR combines a vision encoder, which reads the input image, with a text decoder, which generates the corresponding text.

The pretrained model was fine-tuned on the Urdu image-text dataset prepared during the previous weeks. The training data was loaded in batches and the AdamW optimizer was used to update the model during training.

After training, the model was evaluated on test images that it had not seen during training.

## What I Did

- Loaded the pretrained `microsoft/trocr-base-printed` model.
- Checked GPU availability.
- Created training and testing DataLoaders.
- Configured the AdamW optimizer.
- Trained the model for 3 epochs.
- Evaluated the model on test images.
- Calculated the final accuracy.
- Saved the trained model and processor to Google Drive.

## Results

### Final Accuracy

**0%**

The model did not produce any exact matches between its predictions and the actual text in the test set.

### Prediction Examples

The specific incorrect prediction examples were not recorded.

### Analysis

The 0% exact-match accuracy indicates that the model was not yet able to produce the complete expected Urdu text exactly on the test images.

Possible improvements for future work include collecting more diverse Urdu training data, improving image quality and preprocessing, increasing the amount of training data, and further tuning the model.

## Model

Pretrained model:

`microsoft/trocr-base-printed`

## Training

- Number of epochs: 3
- Batch size: 4
- Optimizer: AdamW
- Learning rate: 5e-5
- Training device: GPU

## Model Saving

The trained model and processor were saved to Google Drive so they could be reused for deployment without retraining from scratch.

